{"name":"Encore","hex":"#6d7383","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"encore","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A brief extra performance, done after the main performance is complete.","A call or demand (as by continued applause) for a repeat performance."]},{"part":"Interjection","definitions":["Please perform again!"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/encore"}],"entries":[{"title":"Encore","blocks":[{"text":"An encore is an additional performance given by performers at the conclusion of a show or concert, usually in response to extended applause from the audience. They are regarded as the most complimentary kind of applause for performers. Multiple encores are not uncommon, and they initially originated spontaneously, when audiences continued to applaud and demand additional performance from the artists after they had left the stage. However, in modern times they are rarely spontaneous and are usually a pre-planned part of the show."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Federspiel_Austrian_World_Music_Awards_2015_Encore_06.jpg/330px-Federspiel_Austrian_World_Music_Awards_2015_Encore_06.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Encore","wikidata":"Q1363276"},{"title":"Encore (Eminem album)","blocks":[{"text":"Encore is the fifth studio album by the American rapper Eminem, released on November 12, 2004, through Aftermath Entertainment, Shady Records, WEB Entertainment, Goliath Artists, and Interscope Records. As reflected in its title, cover art, and certain lyrics, the album was originally set to be Eminem's final studio album. Its lyrical themes include Eminem's criticism of then U.S. president George W. Bush, parodies of singer Michael Jackson and actor Christopher Reeve, and Eminem's feud with rappers Benzino and Ja Rule. Overall, the album features more comedic themes and lyrics than Eminem's previous albums."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/b/b4/Encore_%28Eminem_album%29_coverart.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Encore_(Eminem_album)","wikidata":"Q638412"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Historic_ENCORE_%40_MET.JPG/500px-Historic_ENCORE_%40_MET.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Historic_ENCORE_%40_MET.JPG/1280px-Historic_ENCORE_%40_MET.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Historic_ENCORE_%40_MET.JPG/500px-Historic_ENCORE_%40_MET.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Operatic tenor Javier Camarena finishing a rare encore at the Metropolitan Opera in 2014. Encores are officially banned on the MET stage.[10]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHistoric_ENCORE_%40_MET.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/C_Reeve_in_Marriage_of_Figaro_Opening_night_1985.jpg/330px-C_Reeve_in_Marriage_of_Figaro_Opening_night_1985.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/C_Reeve_in_Marriage_of_Figaro_Opening_night_1985.jpg/330px-C_Reeve_in_Marriage_of_Figaro_Opening_night_1985.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Actor Christopher Reeve is mentioned in several lyrics and was the namesake of an unused track that was later renamed and released on The Death of Slim Shady (Coup de Grâce) (2024).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AC_Reeve_in_Marriage_of_Figaro_Opening_night_1985.jpg"}],"sounds":{"encore":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/59/En-us-enco